{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>We will go up on the highway</b> – Now at first they said they would come through the cities, only they would take care not to enter fields and vineyards, as is the way of large encampments that plunder the granaries and enter the vineyards. But they would go on the king's highway, which is a public road, not a private road. And they said they would not drink their water from the wells at all, for their own needs. Afterward they sent to them that they would not come near the cities at all, but would go on the highway that goes up to the land of Canaan, which is paved and cleared for all, and if they drink the water of the rivers on the roads — they and their livestock, when crossing the river — they would pay their price, for they benefited from them. Therefore he said, \"Only there is nothing\" — that in this there is no loss of anything. And others explained, \"We will not drink water from a well unless for a price,\" but this is not correct, for why would he turn to send him what he did not want at first. And its midrash (Tanhuma, Chukat 12): \"We will not drink from our well, and we will take from you,\" as Rashi wrote (Rashi on Numbers 20:17). And Israel said to Edom, \"Until we pass through your border,\" and did not mention to him <b>unto the land that YHWH our Elohim gives us</b>, lest he be jealous of them concerning the land, saying that it was theirs had he not taken his birthright and his blessing by deception. But to Sihon they mentioned, \"Until I pass over the Jordan into the land that YHWH our Elohim gives us,\" as Moses mentioned in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 2:29)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Numbers 20:17 as ״לא נשתה מי באר אם לא בדמים״, which differs slightly from the Masoretic Text (which reads ״באר מים״); the translation renders his quotation as he cites it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], 